  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nWe will use a multi-start optimization approach combined with mutations and local search. The key idea is to generate multiple initial sequences based on different distributions (Gaussian, uniform, increasing-decreasing) to maximize coverage of the search space. Each sequence is normalized to have a sum of 0.01. The optimization process involves:  \n- Adam optimizer in log-space with cooling schedule to explore deep minima  \n- Linear programming refinement to enforce binding constraints  \n- Local mutations (adding/removing/merging peaks) to escape local optima  \n- Periodic validation using the exact objective function  \nWe assume this is better than gradient descent alone because the objective function is discontinuous and requires exploring directionally different regions.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n- Each candidate sequence a (numpy array) of length n satisfies:  \n  \u2211over j a[j] >= 0.01  \n  a[j] >= 0 for all j  \n- The objective is:  \n  f(a) = (2 * n * \u2225a \u2225_\u221e) / (\u2211 a[j])^2  \n- We will try to minimize f(a)  \n- For each sequence, we compute its full linear convolution (mode=\"full\") of length 2n-1.  \n- The optimization will work in log-space to ensure non-negativity:  \n  Let x = log(a), then a = exp(x)  \n- We use a smoothed version of the objective for gradient computation:  \n  Let h = max(conv(a,a)) / \u2225a\u2225^2, then optimize 2nh + ...  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nWe will define the following steps:  \na. Deadline = time.time() + 1500/1000 * 0 # (convert seconds to parameter space size)  \nb. base_length = 700  \nc. best_seq = None  \nd. best_score = inf  \n\ne. Initialize population:  \n   features = [('uniform', guide_uniform(base_length)), ('increasing', guide_inc_dec(base_length)), ('gaussian', guide_gaussian(base_length, sigma=0.5)), ('comb', guide_comb(base_length, num_peaks=3)), ('comb5', guide_comb(base_length, num_peaks=5)), ('comb10', guide_comb(base_length, num_peaks=10))]  \n\nf. For each template_f in features:  \n   candidate = template_f.copy()  \n   candidate_sum = sum(candidate)  \n   if candidate_sum > 0.01 and not too small:  \n      apply_pruning(candidate) # set values to zero if they become too small (<0.0001)  \n      best_candidate = adam_optimization(candidate)  \n      score = exact_objective(best_candidate)  \n      if score < best_score:  \n          best_seq = best_candidate.copy()  \n          best_score = score  \n\ng. Refinement:  \n   if best_seq is not None:  \n      best_seq = linear_refinement(best_seq, 0.01) # refines with near-optimal TP  \n      best_score = exact_objective(best_seq)  \n\nh. Output:  \n   return best_seq  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n- We will try n around the base_length (700) but will vary from 100 to 1600.  \n- Each candidate is passed through the Adam optimizer with adaptive param.  \n- We use the following templates:  \n  1. Uniform  \n  2. Increasing then decreasing  \n  3. Gaussian peaks  \n  4. Comb with 3 peaks  \n  5. Comb with 5 peaks  \n  6. Comb with 10 peaks  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n- Each Adam optimization will be given a time budget of (total_time * 0.2)  \n- Lin. program refinements: (total_time * 0.1)  \n- We validate each candidate with the exact objective function.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n- Every 200 seconds, save the best candidate so far.  \n- If exact objective returns inf, the candidate is invalid.  \n- We ensure all numbers are in [0, 1000] and sum >= 0.01 before submission.  \n- Final fallback: if no good candidate, return a sequence of length 200 with 1's sticking to 1000.  \n\nhelper function implementation:  \n\ndef guide_uniform(n):  \n    uniform_seq = np.ones(n) * 0.01 / float(n)  \n    return uniform_seq  \n\ndef guide_inc_dec(n):  \n    x = np.linspace(0, 1, n)  \n    peak = int(n/2)  \n    seq = np.maximum(0.0, (x - peak)**2 - 0.5*(n-peak)**2 )  \n    return seq  \n\ndef guide_gaussian(n, sigma=0.5):  \n    center = n//2  \n    g = np.exp( (np.arange(n)-center)**2 / (2*sigma**2) )  \n    return g / sum(g) * 0.01  \n\ndef guide_comb(n, num_peaks):  \n    # num_peaks approximately  \n    positions = sorted(np.random.choice(n, num_peaks, replace=False))  \n    values = np.exp( (positions - center)**2 / (2*(n//2)**2) )  \n    comb_seq = np.zeros(n)  \n    comb_seq[positions] = values / sum(values) * 0.01  \n    return comb_seq  \n\ndef adam_optimization(x):  \n    # uses the Adam optimizer from the previous code, slightly modified  \n    ...  \n\ndef linear_refinement(x, target_sum=0.01):  \n    # solves an LP to adjust the sequence to minimal peak while \u2211=0.01  \n    ...  \n\ndef exact_objective(a):  \n    # compares with the given function for this task  \n    ...  \n\ndef apply_pruning(candidate):  \n    # reduces small values to near-zero  \n    ...  \n\nWe must be sure to pass the exact objective through the optimizer at key points.",
